abstract: >-
  An open evaluation framework for LLM-based systems answering consumer-facing
  U.S. public-health questions (CDC, NIH, FDA, CMS) in English and Spanish:
  a pre-registered protocol, a five-dimension scoring rubric, an error taxonomy
  for consumer health AI, and a scoring implementation. Released alongside a
  post-freeze defect audit of the 2,020-item benchmark the framework produced,
  which passed 2,020/2,020 schema and source validation yet contains template
  collisions with contradicting gold answers (28.0% of items), encoding
  corruption (17.1% overall, 74.9% of Spanish items), splice contamination and
  source pollution. Re-scoring under two matching rules shows the resulting
  score distortion has no fixed direction: its sign is set by the scorer's
  matching rule rather than by the defect. The item set is released as
  provisional and is not fit for scoring systems; the transferable contribution
  is the defect taxonomy, the detection procedures, and the audit code.
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Schema Validity Is Not Semantic Validity: A Defect Taxonomy for Template-Generated Evaluation Benchmarks"
    authors:
